# ENGINE-UNIVERSAL-SERVICES.17 slice 4 — G4: the trailing guard is CALL-SITE scoped, measured.
#
# `g3_trailing_guard.ebnf` plus a SECOND holder of `prim` — one with NO residual, which therefore
# wants the whole run:
#
#   G3:  scratch := kw_k eq outer_cast semi
#   G4:  scratch := kw_k eq outer_cast semi | kw_k eq prim semi
#                                           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the residual-free holder
#
# REQUIRED VERDICT: **REJECT** on `e7` (`k = n;`). `prim` matches `n`, then the trailing guard
# demands a `tick lparen lit rparen` that this holder never wanted and the input does not carry, so
# `prim` fails and BOTH alternatives die with it.
#
# ⛔⛔ WHAT IT PINS. This is slice 1's Q5 law at the new position: a follow restriction written onto
# the RULE cannot be right for two holders that disagree about the residual. `constant_primary` must
# reserve a trailing `' ( … )` when it is reached from `cast`, and must NOT when it is reached from
# an ordinary expression — and `constant_primary` is reached from both. ⇒ the trailing guard belongs
# on the same sheared clone the loop guard does, never on the shared rule. The transformation
# therefore buys the clone chain ONCE and hangs both guards on it; it does not buy a second one.
#
# ⛔ `e1` must still ACCEPT here — the first holder is unharmed. A file that rejected everything
# would prove nothing about scoping. `g5_trailing_guard_clone_control.ebnf` is the one-difference
# control that proves `e7` is parseable at all under this shape.
@entry: true
scratch := kw_k eq outer_cast semi
         | kw_k eq prim semi
outer_cast := ct tick lparen lit rparen
ct := kw | prim
prim := prim_base ( prim_suffix &( tick lparen lit rparen ) )* &( tick lparen lit rparen )
prim_base := lit | cast_seed
cast_seed := kw tick lparen lit rparen
prim_suffix := tick lparen lit rparen

lit := trivia /n/
kw := trivia /t/
kw_k := trivia /k/
eq := trivia "="
semi := trivia ";"
tick := trivia "'"
lparen := trivia "("
rparen := trivia ")"

@branch_policy: priority_first
trivia := (line_comment | block_comment)*
line_comment := /\/\/[^\n]*(\n|$)/
block_comment := /\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\//
